+- The 2026-04-20 local-payment-method transition says incomplete customer authorization after 48 hours moves the PaymentIntent from `requires_action` to `requires_payment_method` and permits another confirmation attempt. The 2026-11-07 card-oriented transition lists `succeeded`, `requires_payment_method` and `requires_action`, with possible 3DS for incomplete authorization. It requires server retrieval or server webhook verification and omits the 48-hour example. A browser return alone does not verify status.
+- **Webhooks.** The mapping has no replacement for `source.chargeable`, `source.failed` or `source.canceled`. Both `charge.succeeded` and `charge.failed` are still sent, and `charge.dispute.created` stays in both mappings. The PaymentIntent mapping uses `payment_intent.succeeded` and `payment_intent.payment_failed`. The Checkout comparison names `payment_intent.succeeded` for payment success, and the Checkout event mapping names `checkout.session.completed` for Session completion. A completed Session can have a pending payment. The customer can retry payment within the same Session until expiry, when `checkout.session.expired` is sent. Verify the paid outcome server-side. See April lines 26–32 and 60–71, November lines 27–33 and 63–74, and [[stripe-checkout#Key Webhooks|Checkout webhook handling]].
+- **Reusable local Sources.** The April local-method guide says saved Alipay, Bacs Direct Debit and SEPA Direct Debit Sources do not migrate automatically. It describes converting them with the Dashboard data migration tool. The November transition overview omits this compatibility section.
+- **Saved legacy cards**. The 2026-04-20 transition describes a compatible saved Card as a Payment Methods view of the same object and shows its ID in `payment_method` with the matching Customer. The separate November detailed card migration says unmigrated Card/Source IDs can no longer be passed in `payment_method`, and describes the transitional PaymentIntent `source` parameter, explicitly undocumented, plus `customer` if attached. Its tool for migrating existing Cards to PaymentMethods is private preview. The November transition overview also omits the April saved-card compatibility section and points to the distinct detailed card guide.
+- **Charge IDs, refunds and errors.** The transition guide describes retaining Charge references and also recommends recording the PaymentIntent ID. Both captures accept either a PaymentIntent ID or the Charge ID from `latest_charge` for a refund request. April presents the Charge ID first, and November presents the PaymentIntent ID first. Both allow either identifier. Verify refund status separately. Inspect PaymentIntent errors at creation and after customer authorization. The codes can differ from corresponding Sources errors. See April lines 34–58 and November lines 35–61.
 
 ## Integration Upgrade Map
 
-| Legacy | Recommended | Key benefit |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Card Element | Payment Element | 100+ payment methods; dynamic presentment by location/currency |
-| Legacy Checkout | Checkout or Payment Links | Adaptive Pricing, Dynamic payment methods, embed or redirect |
-| Payment Request Button | Express Checkout Element | Apple Pay + Amazon Pay + Google Pay + Link + PayPal simultaneously |
-| Manual payment methods | Dynamic payment methods | Dashboard-managed; Stripe selects eligible methods per transaction |
+ | Legacy | Recommended | Key benefit | 
+ | --- | --- | --- | 
+ | Card Element | Payment Element | 100+ payment methods; dynamic presentment by location/currency | 
+ | Legacy Checkout | Checkout or Payment Links | Adaptive Pricing, Dynamic payment methods, embed or redirect | 
+ | Payment Request Button | Express Checkout Element | Apple Pay + Amazon Pay + Google Pay + Link + PayPal simultaneously | 
+ | Manual payment methods | Dynamic payment methods | Dashboard-managed; Stripe selects eligible methods per transaction | 
 
 The 2026-11-07 [Payment Request Button page](https://docs.stripe.com/stripe-js/elements/payment-request-button) marks the Element deprecated and directs readers to Express Checkout Element and its migration guide. The [2026-09-30 Endive changelog](https://docs.stripe.com/changelog/endive/2026-09-30/deprecate-payment-request-button) says Stripe.js through Dahlia supports the Element and that `elements.create('paymentRequestButton')` throws starting with Endive, including React `PaymentRequestButtonElement` integrations. The change concerns the Element. See [[stripe-express-checkout-element#Migrating from Payment Request Button Element|Express Checkout Element migration]].
 
